Definition <p><code>fork()</code> creates a child process that is an exact copy of the parent — same code, same data, same open files — except the return value.</p> <pre><code>pid_t pid = fork();

if (pid == 0) {
    // child process (fork returned 0)
    printf("I am the child (pid=%d)\n", getpid());
} else if (pid &gt; 0) {
    // parent process (fork returned child's pid)
    printf("I am the parent of %d\n", pid);
} else {
    // fork failed
    perror("fork");
}
</code></pre>   ???+ note "What gets copied?"     - **Text** (code) — usually shared (read-only).     - **Data** — copied (copy-on-write optimisation).     - **Heap** — copied.     - **Stack** — copied.     - **File descriptors** — copied (both share the same file offsets).     - **PCB** — copied and given a new PID.     !!! tip "Copy-on-write"     Modern kernels do not physically copy memory at `fork()`. The parent and child share the same physical pages, marked read-only. When either writes, that page is copied (COW). This makes `fork()` very fast for the "copy-then-exec" pattern.

Two threads incrementing a counter <pre><code>// shared: int counter = 0
void* worker(void* arg) {
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; N; i++)
        counter++;
    return NULL;
}
</code></pre>   ???+ info "The machine code"     <pre><code>load  R1, counter
add   R1, R1, 1
store R1, counter
</code></pre>   ???+ note "The interleaving"     | Time | Thread A | Thread B | Memory |     |---|---|---|---|     | 1 | load R1, 0 | | 0 |     | 2 | add R1, 1 | | 0 |     | 3 | | load R2, 0 | 0 |     | 4 | | add R2, 1 | 0 |     | 5 | store R1, 1 | | 1 |     | 6 | | store R2, 1 | 1 |      **Both threads incremented the counter. Final value = 1.** One increment is **lost**.     ???+ success "The consequence"     If both threads each increment 1,000,000 times, the final counter may be **anywhere from 1,000,000 to 2,000,000** — depending on the timing.

Definition <p>The critical section is the code segment that accesses shared data. The critical-section problem is to design a protocol so that:</p>   ???+ abstract "Three requirements (Silberschatz §6.2)"     **1. Mutual exclusion.** At most one process may be in its critical section at a time.      **2. Progress.** If no process is in the critical section, and some are waiting to enter, only those wishing to enter participate in the decision — and the decision cannot be postponed indefinitely.      **3. Bounded waiting.** There is a bound on the number of times other processes can enter their critical sections after a process has requested entry and before it is granted.     ???+ note "The skeleton"     <pre><code>do {
    // ENTRY section — request permission
    // CRITICAL section — access shared data
    // EXIT section — release permission
    // REMAINDER section — non-critical code
} while (true);
</code></pre>   !!! warning "All three are required"     Mutual exclusion alone is not enough — a process could starve. Bounded waiting alone is not enough — two processes could enter simultaneously. All three must hold.

The setup (two processes, \(P_0\) and \(P_1\)) <p>Two shared variables: - <code>int turn</code> — whose turn it is. - <code>bool flag[2]</code> — which process wants to enter.</p>   ???+ note "The algorithm"     <pre><code>// Process Pi (i = 0 or 1), j = 1 - i
do {
    flag[i] = true;           // I want to enter
    turn = j;                 // but give the turn to the other
    while (flag[j] &amp;&amp; turn == j) ; // wait while other wants in AND has the turn
    // CRITICAL SECTION
    flag[i] = false;          // I'm done
    // REMAINDER SECTION
} while (true);
</code></pre>   ???+ info "Why it works"     - **Mutual exclusion.** If both are in the critical section, both `flag` are true. `turn` has one value — say 0. Then $P_1$ must have exited the loop (because $turn = 1$ was true for $P_1$, but `turn` is now 0). Contradiction.     - **Progress.** If $P_0$ wants to enter and $P_1$ does not, $flag[1]$ = false → $P_0$ enters immediately.     - **Bounded waiting.** After $P_1$ exits, it sets `flag[1] = false`; $P_0$ then enters. $P_0$ waits at most one entry by $P_1$.     !!! warning "Not practical on modern CPUs"     Peterson's solution assumes **sequential consistency** of memory. Modern CPUs and compilers **reorder** operations. Without memory barriers, it fails. It is pedagogically valuable but not used in production.

The setup <ul> <li>A bounded buffer of size \(N\).</li> <li>Producers insert items into the buffer.</li> <li>Consumers remove items from the buffer.</li> <li>Constraint: producer must wait if buffer is full; consumer must wait if buffer is empty.</li> </ul>   ???+ note "Three semaphores (Silberschatz §6.6)"     | Semaphore | Initial | Meaning |     |---|---|---|     | **mutex** | 1 | Protects the buffer |     | **empty** | $N$ | Counts empty slots |     | **full** | 0 | Counts filled slots |     ???+ note "The producer"     <pre><code>do {
    item = produce();
    wait(empty);          // wait for an empty slot
    wait(mutex);          // enter critical section
    buffer[in] = item;
    in = (in + 1) % N;
    signal(mutex);        // exit critical section
    signal(full);         // signal a filled slot
} while (true);
</code></pre>   ???+ note "The consumer"     <pre><code>do {
    wait(full);           // wait for a filled slot
    wait(mutex);          // enter critical section
    item = buffer[out];
    out = (out + 1) % N;
    signal(mutex);        // exit critical section
    signal(empty);        // signal an empty slot
    consume(item);
} while (true);
</code></pre>   !!! warning "The order of wait() calls matters"     Producer does `wait(empty)` **before** `wait(mutex)`. If reversed, a full buffer would deadlock — the producer would hold `mutex` while waiting for `empty`, and the consumer could never acquire `mutex` to free a slot.

Definition (Hoare 1974) <p>A monitor is a synchronization construct that bundles: - Shared data. - Procedures that operate on the data. - A mutex that ensures only one process is active in the monitor at a time.</p>   ???+ note "The structure"     <pre><code>monitor Buffer {
    int[] data;
    int count;

    procedure insert(item):
        // only one thread in here at a time
        ...

    procedure remove():
        // only one thread in here at a time
        ...
}
</code></pre>   ???+ note "Condition variables"     Monitors include **condition variables** for waiting:     - `cond.wait()` — suspend the calling process; release the monitor.     - `cond.signal()` — wake one waiting process.     !!! tip "Why monitors are easier"     Semaphores require you to **remember the order** of `wait`/`signal` calls — a wrong order causes deadlock or corruption. Monitors **enforce** mutual exclusion automatically — you just call procedures.

The setup <ul> <li>Readers access the shared resource without modifying it.</li> <li>Writers modify it.</li> <li>Multiple readers can access simultaneously.</li> <li>A writer requires exclusive access.</li> </ul>   ???+ note "Two variants"     | Variant | Preference | Starvation risk |     |---|---|---|     | **First readers-writers** | Reader-priority | Writers may starve |     | **Second readers-writers** | Writer-priority | Readers may starve |     | **Fair** | FIFO | None |     ???+ note "Reader-priority solution (Silberschatz)"     <pre><code>sem_t rw_mutex;       // 1, shared between readers and writers
sem_t mutex;          // 1, protects read_count
int read_count = 0;

// Reader
wait(mutex);
read_count++;
if (read_count == 1) wait(rw_mutex);
signal(mutex);
// READ
wait(mutex);
read_count--;
if (read_count == 0) signal(rw_mutex);
signal(mutex);

// Writer
wait(rw_mutex);
// WRITE
signal(rw_mutex);
</code></pre>   !!! warning "Writers can starve"     If readers keep arriving, `read_count` never reaches 0, and the writer never acquires `rw_mutex`. **Writer-priority** solves this by having arriving writers block new readers.

The setup <p>Five philosophers around a table. Five forks between them. Each philosopher needs two forks to eat.</p> <pre><code>        P0
       /  \
      F0   F4
     /      \
    P1      P4
    |        |
    F1      F3
     \      /
       P2-P3
</code></pre>   ???+ note "The naïve solution"     <pre><code>// Philosopher i
do {
    wait(fork[i]);              // pick up left
    wait(fork[(i+1) % 5]);      // pick up right
    eat();
    signal(fork[i]);
    signal(fork[(i+1) % 5]);
} while (true);
</code></pre>   !!! warning "The deadlock"     If all five pick up their left fork simultaneously, all five wait for their right fork forever. **Circular wait** — one of the four Coffman conditions (Chapter 5).     ???+ note "Solution 1 — asymmetric"     Philosopher $i$ picks up the **left** fork first if $i$ is even, the **right** first if $i$ is odd. This breaks the circular-wait chain.     ???+ note "Solution 2 — limit concurrency"     A semaphore `seats = 4`. Each philosopher does `wait(seats)` before picking forks. At most 4 philosophers compete → at least one pair of forks is free.     ???+ note "Solution 3 — atomic pickup"     A monitor with `pickup(i)` and `putdown(i)`. `pickup` acquires both forks atomically or waits on a condition variable.     ???+ success "**Which is best?**"     The **monitor solution** is cleanest. The **asymmetric** solution is clever but non-obvious. The **concurrency limit** is simple but reduces throughput.

The algorithm <pre><code>while (!(status &amp; READY)) ;
data = device.data;
</code></pre>   ???+ note "Trace"     | Time | CPU | Device |     |---|---|---|     | 0 | issues read command | starts |     | 1 | poll: not ready | working |     | 2 | poll: not ready | working |     | ... | ... | ... |     | 1000 | poll: ready | done |     | 1001 | read data | — |     ???+ note "Cost analysis"     - For a 10 ms disk read: the CPU wastes 10⁷ cycles polling (at 1 GHz).     - For a 1 µs device read: the CPU wastes 1,000 cycles — acceptable.     !!! warning "When polling is a mistake"     Any device slower than the CPU (most) makes polling a disaster. The CPU should not busy-wait. But polling is fine for **very fast devices** (network cards at 10 Gbps) or in **bare-metal embedded systems** with no interrupt controller.
